{
  "claimable": true,
  "schema_version": 1,
  "name": "approvals",
  "description": "Human-in-the-loop approval binding. `action_cases` pin the canonical approval-action document and its digest: the facts that make one approvable action different from another, so a grant issued for one call cannot be spent on a different call that happens to trip the same rule. What the digest binds is the rule id, the rule definition hash, the action name, the amount, the caller and target namespaces, and the subject; what it deliberately does NOT bind is the free-text prompt, because an approved request and its retry differ in it more often than not and a binding that voids itself on a whitespace change is one operators switch off. `match_cases` pin the grant-matching rule as (grant, claim) -> satisfied: each pin only NARROWS - when both the grant and the call carry a value they must match, when either is silent the check does not apply - which is what lets a grant issuer adopt the bindings one at a time, and is also why an unbound grant is exactly as strong as the issuer made it. A case may declare `same_as` to name another case it is intentionally identical to; every other pair must hash differently, which is the property the digest exists for. Harnesses: sdk-typescript/tests/unit/approvals-conformance.test.ts, sdk-python/tests/test_approvals_conformance.py.",
